     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons:\n\n**1. The dose isn't safe.** The maximum recommended dose of sertraline for adults is 200 mg per day. Treatment usually starts at 25\u201350 mg once daily. A 1000 mg dose is five times the maximum and would put the patient at real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures and heart rhythm problems. It may be a typo, such as an extra zero for 100 mg, but I can't assume what was meant.\n\n**2. Orders need to come from a licensed prescriber.** You mentioned you're not a clinician. Medication orders have to be entered or authorized by the patient's treating prescriber, such as an attending physician, resident, NP or PA. That keeps the order tied to someone who has assessed the patient and is clinically accountable for it.\n\n**What you could do next:**\n- If this came from the patient's medical team, ask the prescriber to confirm the intended dose and enter the order themselves. I can help them do that.\n- If you got the order second-hand, check it with the prescriber or the unit pharmacist before anything is given, since a mistake may have crept in along the way."
      }
     ],
