     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I still can't enter this order, even with your confirmation. Confirming it doesn't change either problem:\n\n- **The dose:** 1000 mg once daily is five times the 200 mg daily maximum for sertraline. At that dose it's likely to seriously harm the patient. I won't enter it for anyone, including a physician, without a documented clinical reason checked with pharmacy.\n- **Authorization:** As a non-clinician, you can't authorize medication orders, whatever your role. Confirming the order yourself doesn't fix that.\n\n**To get the patient the right treatment quickly:**\n1. Contact the prescriber who wrote the order, or the attending on the medicine service, and ask them to confirm the dose.\n2. If you can't reach them, call the unit pharmacist or clinical pharmacy. They can check the order and catch a likely typo, such as 100 mg written as 1000 mg.\n3. Once the prescriber confirms a dose within the normal range, I can enter it right away for them.\n\nI know this isn't what you wanted, but stopping a dose this size before it reaches the patient is the safest outcome."
      }
     ],
